I need you to be in touch. Get in touch this week, won't you?

53
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
So we've got a pretty full slate for this podcast,

54
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
So let's get started

55
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
In seven minutes or less.

56
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Last week I answered an email question about a young troop, new scout master, brand new troop,

57
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
As a matter of fact-

58
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
And the question basically came down to: you know, until these guys have a little bit more experience,

59
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
should I just be leading everything?

60
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:43,000
And my answer was: well, no, you should be enabling them to lead themselves.

61
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
And this kind of got me to thinking about this one question:

62
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
What are the qualifications for a youth leader?

63
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
What are the qualifications for a youth leader?

64
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Dial back, Let's go back about 25 years.

65
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
I'm a young scout master. I want to do this right.

66
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
I've been trained, I've gone through all this stuff,

67
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
I've listened to what a lot of people had to say,

68
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
I've read things, And so I decided: to have a good troop,

69
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
We have to have really capable youth leaders.

70
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And to have really capable youth leaders, we need to have some qualifications.

71
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
So I sit down and I'm used about it for a little while,

72
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
I ask around and we think, okay, well, you know,

73
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
we're going to talk about maybe having the qualification for patrol leader.

74
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
The patrol leader needs to be first class or above,

75
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And the senior patrol leader needs to be at least a star scout or above,

76
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
And a patrol leader needs to be at least 13..

77
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
And maybe the senior patrol leader needs to be at least 14..

78
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
And so, you know, we get out the typewriter at the time and we, you know, put

79
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and we put this into effect and we make a policy,

80
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
And then we wait anxiously for things to get better.

81
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Hey, we wrote a policy and it's designed to make things better,

82
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
So that means that in the next six months things are going to get better.

83
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
I'm trying to put our expectations into policy statements and into job descriptions

84
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
and things like that.

85
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
It's not going to work.

86
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Let me stretch my basketball analogy again.

87
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
You know, you go join a basketball team and you put somebody into the center forward spot.

88
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Do you have them sign a contract?

89
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
No,

90
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Do you have them read a job description of what the center forward does?

91
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Does anybody ever do that?

92
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
I don't think so.

93
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And it's got to be the same type of thing with scouting leadership positions, to my mind.

94
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,000
And very shortly after I wrote those qualifications, there were anomalies.

95
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Hey, there was a guy who was maybe 12 years old, who would make a really great patrol leader,

96
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
And there was a guy who was maybe 13 years old who would make a really great senior patrol leader

97
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
And that's who everybody wanted to have.

98
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And why couldn't we go ahead and do that?

99
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And yeah, he wasn't quite star yet, but he would be star in a month or two, or he wasn't quite first class yet,

100
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
But you know he was. and then you start looking at the policy and you start thinking maybe this isn't such a good idea.

101
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
And it isn't.

102
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
You can't create things.

103
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:19,000
You can't create what you want by writing a policy or a procedural or a job description.

104
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
You can't create that thing.

105
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
You created a different way.

106
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,000
If none of these qualifications worked, for me what works?

107
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,000
What is the qualification for a boy to become a youth leader,

108
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
To enter into that progression of developing skills and things towards leadership?

109
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
What qualification must he have?

110
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:48,000
And the only qualification is that he is a scout,

111
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
That's it.

112
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
It doesn't matter if he's been there for one week, one month or three or four years.

113
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
He is qualified to become a leader

114
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,000
And he should be able to nominate himself for election to any leadership position that's going to be elect.

115
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,000
To my mind, the qualification for leadership is: is he a scout?

116
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Yes, does he want to do it,

117
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Yes.

118
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Well, there you go.

119
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
And then we work with whatever raw material the scouts give us through their elections.

120
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,000
If, if they elect an 11 year old patrol leader, they elect an 11 year old patrol leader, we work with that.

121
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
We do what we have to do.

122
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:31,000
If we have somebody who's just a total raw recruit, never seen very much in the way of a Boy Scout troop, and he becomes a patrol leader,

123
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
What are we going to do?

124
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:44,000
Well, the first thing we're not going to do is we're not going to walk him through the 75 points of being a patrol leader and a lengthy job description

125
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:49,000
about exactly what he's going to have, what. what's going to happen next?

126
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
How about letting him just do this in small bites?

127
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Let's not have him drink from a fire hose,

128
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Okay,

129
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Let's just let him discover this job in small bites.

130
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:11,000
And maybe he needs a little bit more mentoring than that 14 year old who's a star scout who just got elected to be a patrol leader.

131
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Maybe he needs a little bit more mentoring in that wise.

132
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:23,000
And maybe there's a guy in the troop, an older scout, junior assistant scout, master, who can take the time and work with him step by step.

133
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:28,000
But he'll go to his first patrol meeting and he'll have no idea what to have.

134
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
What do I do now?

135
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Well, you got to take- let's have you take- attendance and collect dues and check uniforms.

136
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
How about that?

137
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
That's pretty easy.

138
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
So he'll give that a shot.

139
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
And we start talking about a camping trip coming up.

140
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
Well, what do you have to do to get ready for the camping trip?

141
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,000
I'm not really sure.

142
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Well, what are you going to sleep in?

143
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
I guess we're going to sleep in a tent.

144
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Where do the tents come from?

145
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
The troop has them, right?

146
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Yep, the troop has them.

147
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Now, how are you going to get them and get them to where you're going to go and take care of them while they're there and make sure they get back to the troop?

148
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Well, I guess somebody will be responsible to do that.

149
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Well, do you know what the name of that person is?

150
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Oh, that's that quartermaster thing. right,

151
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
I read about that,

152
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
I saw that somewhere.

153
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,000
I heard about that.

154
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Yeah, that's what it is.

155
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
How are you going to eat?

156
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Well, I guess somebody's going to have to buy the food and stuff like that.

157
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Well, how are you going to make that happen?

158
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Question after question after question,

159
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
And you know. finally he becomes a patrol leader.

160
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,000
That's much

161
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Now. I've already talked about that last week, but this is this is what I think is real important.

162
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:47,000
We have the misapprehension that because we're the adult, because we are the mature, skilled leader, because we're the mature, skilled instructor,

163
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
that we're the only ones that should be doing that.

164
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Well, that, no, no, no. that's not the way that it works,

165
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:00,000
The way the program is written. Scouts get these broad, wonderful, comprehensive opportunities,

166
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:08,000
And what we do is we take and we build on each successive step they make in competence.

167
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:23,000
And we do that not by doling out progressive responsibility to them, but by giving them encouragement and information each step of the way.

168
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:28,000
They're fully responsible when they have the name patrol leader, they're fully responsible,

169
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
And so we work along with them.

170
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Each scout is going to progress through these levels of competency individually, at an individual speed.

171
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
There's no regimentation about this at all.

172
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Some patrol leaders will develop very quickly.

173
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Some will develop very quickly in one area and not very much in another.

174
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Some of them will develop a little more slowly across all areas.

175
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
You, just you. each one of them is an individual,

176
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:56,000
And so our style has to fit each individual:

177
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:00,000
The mentoring and the things that go on need to fit each individual scout.

178
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:05,000
And so we have to talk to them- and, you know, not in the middle of their patrol meeting,

179
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,000
not in the middle of a camp out or an activity that they're running-

180
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,000
but we do need to speak with them and we need to get an idea of what's going on and we need to observe.

181
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
And that's the method,

182
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
That's the process of scouting.

183
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
So here's the two or three typical scenarios that I get.

184
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
First of all, the first scenario is: I have a brand new troop.

185
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,000
They're all young.

186
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
Nobody knows how to do anything.

187
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,000
What do I do next?

188
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Well, I think I told you just now.

189
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:37,000
The next one is: I have 30 new young scouts.

190
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
I have 15 older scouts.

191
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
The older scouts don't seem to be very interested in doing any of the leadership,

192
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
but the young scouts are all willing to do it

193
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:50,000
And the older guys are just sitting back not doing much.

194
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Well, that's fine,

195
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
You know. if the older guys aren't interested in leadership and they're not interested in advancement

196
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and they just want to go camping and they're not making trouble, let them be.

197
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:07,000
If they're going to elect themselves a 11 or 12 year old senior patrol leader, that's fine with me.

198
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
You know we work with the resources that the scouts bring to us.

199
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
We don't get to pick and choose necessarily which those resources are going to be.

200
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
We're going to work with whatever resources they bring to us

201
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
And we're going to base it at the patrol.

202
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,000
So get patrol leaders elected and start working with each one of them individually.

203
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:32,000
If you're big enough to have more than one patrol, then you get a senior patrol leader in there

204
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,000
And you just start working with it little by little over time. but you do it at a boy scale,

205
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
not an adult scale.

206
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
You do it at their scale and their speed

207
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:42,000
And it's going to work.

208
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,000
It has to work.

209
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Baden Powell said it would work.

210
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:53,000
He said that the patrol method is the only method and it cannot fail if you apply it properly.

211
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:54,000
I got to agree with him.

212
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
I think it definitely worked.

213
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods,

214
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods,

215
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Write me a letter, send it by name

216
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Email, that is folks.

217
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails

218
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:56,000
From Mark Bowie, scout master of troop 531 in Orange, California.

219
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
He says: hello, Clark,

220
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
I enjoy your thoughtful blogs and podcasts.

221
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Well, thank you, Mark,

222
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:05,000
And I'm interested to learn about your views on Meribadge days.

223
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:10,000
Now, what I'm understanding is Meribadge day is kind of like Meribadge college

224
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:18,000
or any kind of an event where multiple Meribadges are offered with counselors attached.

225
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:25,000
I know our local museum near us offers two or three Meribadges a couple of three days a year

226
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,000
And scouts can come in and as a group they can work their way through the Meribadge.

227
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:38,000
He says your podcast item about Baden Powell's view on badge hunters versus badge earners was timely,

228
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:42,000
And we have recently been having a debate in our troop about Meribadge days.

229
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,000
One side says that the primary purpose is to raise money for the hosting troop

230
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:54,000
And I don't really know how that works, but I'll accept the fact that somewhere out there a troop is hosting a Meribadge day

231
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
and they might be doing it as a fundraiser

232
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:02,000
And not to ensure quality instruction for the boys, and that they're only a short cut on Meribadge completion.

233
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:10,000
Others say that in today's business schedule is too hard to find Meribadge counselor and schedule meetings with them to review progress

234
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:15,000
And that Meribadge days are the only practical way outside of summer camp to complete Meribadges.

235
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:16,000
What do you think?

236
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Well, I'm going to stand directly in the middle because I agree.

237
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
I agree with both sides to a certain extent.

238
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
So let me explain myself.

239
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:36,000
First of all, Meribadges: there's a very simple system and there's a very simple rules about who gets involved. where

240
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:40,000
The boy brings you a blank blue card, Mr Scoutmaster,

241
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,000
He says: I am going to do, you know, camping Meribadge.

242
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,000
And I would ask who is your counselor?

243
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
And I would say: is he a certified counselor?

244
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:56,000
And if I needed to check up on that, I would do what resources I needed to make sure that this was a registered Meribadge counselor

245
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:05,000
And I would possibly review with the boy the youth protection specifications around Meribadges.

246
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,000
There are no one-on-one meetings with adults.

247
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
In other words, bring a scout with you or there needs to be a second adult present

248
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
And then I would pat him on his head and send him on his happy way.

249
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Several days, weeks, months later he will bring me a completed blue card.

250
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:26,000
I will tell him to take it to the advancement chairman or the troop scribe

251
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:32,000
And then the troop scribe or the advancement chairman will hand me the blue card to sign at one point

252
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:43,000
And I will hand the scout a Meribadge at our earliest convenience and shake his hand and congratulate him for a job well done.

253
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:48,000
Now, nowhere in there, nowhere in that very simple process,

254
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:53,000
does the scout master interfere with who the counselor is,

255
00:17:53,000 --> 00:18:00,000
If it's a registered Meribadge counselor, the rigor of the study of the Meribadge or the quality of the instruction of the Meribadge

256
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
or the quality of work that a scout does with the Meribadge.

257
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:08,000
I see only blue cards. I see happy scouts. I hand out Meribadges.

258
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:17,000
If, for some reason, I was to become concerned about the instruction of a given Meribadge,

259
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:23,000
or I was to become concerned that, you know, somebody was just giving Meribadges away-

260
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:30,000
which is a concern that's often expressed, but in 25 years I have never, ever seen anybody giving Meribadges away-

261
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:35,000
then I would take that concern to the people who are certifying Meribadge counselors,

262
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Because it is not the scout's fault if the Meribadge counselor is not doing his job.

263
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
It's the Meribadge counselor's fault.

264
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Who certifies Meribadge counselors? Well, your council advancement committee does

265
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,000
and your district advancement committee, and that would be the person I would take the concerns to.

266
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:57,000
If you really think, if people are really thinking that these Meribadge days, as you call them,

267
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:03,000
are just shortcuts, and take it to the next level and ask what kind of quality control

268
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
is being done for the counselors that are operating there and things like that.

269
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,000
Otherwise, keep your nose out and let the scouts go and do and achieve.

270
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,000
Meribadge is not a certificate of completion of a course of rigorous study. I gotta tell you,

271
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:22,000
A scout with life-saving Meribadge is not a qualified life-saver,

272
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:25,000
nor is a scout with first-aid Meribadge and EMT.

273
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,000
Those kind of certifications require far more rigorous study.

274
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:32,000
First-aid Meribadge and life-saving Meribadge are valuable and they're a valuable set of skills,

275
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:40,000
But we would never depend on them as a sole indication of somebody's skill level, maturity

276
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:45,000
and their ability to handle a situation that a lifeguard or an EMT would handle, would we?

277
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Now I am going to differ with the others that say in today's busy schedules

278
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
it's too hard to find a Meribadge counselor in schedule meetings and things like that.

279
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
Well, we have things like email.

280
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:00,000
I have done significant parts of Meribadges that I counsel with email.

281
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,000
My inner drill sergeant would like to see more rigor in study

282
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and more rigor in the instruction of Meribadges,

283
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:12,000
but understanding what Meribadges represent, what they're supposed to represent

284
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:18,000
and how scouts will have learned a little something about a given field of study or endeavor.

285
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
I'm quite satisfied with the way that the system works.

286
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
I will have a little internal dialogue with the internal drill sergeant

287
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and I will say, you know it's not that big a deal.

288
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Baden Powell said something about the jolity of scouting.

289
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:35,000
Jolity is not a word that you hear a whole lot,

290
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:39,000
but you know Baden Powell was born in 1857, so let's cut him a break.

291
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:45,000
That indicates to me a certain amount of lightness and fun

292
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:49,000
and intensity and excitement surrounding the work of scouting.

293
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
I wouldn't be too upset about the Meribadges days.

294
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:52,000
I kind of agree.

295
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:53,000
I'm a little suspicious of the whole thing.

296
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:57,000
I'm suspicious of, you know, 30 guys and a 16-year-old counselor

297
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
trying to complete swimming Meribadge at camp.

298
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:04,000
Just how rigorous and complete can the instruction be in that kind of a situation?

299
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
But it's been going on for years

300
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and you know I'm satisfied that the guys have gotten out of it

301
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
what they need to get out of it.

302
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
These are not my badges to protect,

303
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
They're not my standards to protect.

304
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,000
I have yet to embroider, hand, embroider a Meribadge

305
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
and hand it out at a court of honor.

306
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
We go and buy them from the Boy Scouts of America

307
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
who own the Meribadge and who own the program,

308
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:30,000
And I will assent to the way that they're going to administer it,

309
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:34,000
even though the inter-drill sergeant may have some difficulties with it.

310
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:35,000
So I hope that helps you, Mark,

311
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
And if you need more clarification on that, do drop me a line.

312
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
This has to be the truth, folks,

313
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:49,000
because there is no way anyone could make this up.

314
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
So I've been a Scout Master long enough

315
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:00,000
that my career stretches back to the Beavis and Butthead age.

316
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:06,000
If you don't know what I'm referring to when I speak of Beavis and Butthead,

317
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:10,000
consider yourself lucky and move on.

318
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:27,000
Beavis and Butthead were characters in a cartoon show.

319
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:33,000
They were your quintessential teenage kind of dopey kids

320
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:41,000
And they had catchphrases and spoke in this kind of adolescent code

321
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:46,000
And I never really cared for them personally.

322
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:50,000
I mean, I see, you know it was Mike Judge and the guys.

323
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:54,000
They were, of course, very good writers and everything like that,

324
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:58,000
But Beavis and Butthead, for one reason or another, just drove me crazy.

325
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:05,000
Now it's important to realize that it's the job of each succeeding generation

326
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:08,000
to drive the older folks a little nutty. right,

327
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
You did it and I did it.

328
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:14,000
So I'm not making like a condemnation of anybody here,

329
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:17,000
But I had a couple of guys in the Scout Troop

330
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
And God bless them,

331
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:24,000
They were the biggest Beavis and Butthead fans you would ever want to meet

332
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:41,000
And they assumed the characters of Beavis and Butthead

333
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:46,000
And they just were pretty much 24-7.

334
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
They did not break character,

335
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:49,000
They spoke like them,

336
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
They acted like them

337
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:57,000
And it just got so very tiresome to hear all little catchphrases

338
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,000
and the attitude and things like that.

339
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:04,000
Well, we struggled with that for a couple of years

340
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:09,000
And, you know, the worst thing you can do with something like that

341
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:12,000
is draw attention to it by telling them to stop.

342
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:15,000
You know, they have to live their own lives.

343
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,000
It wasn't improper that, it was just aggregate.

344
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
But I'll tell you something:

345
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:25,000
I found a secret and I found out how to make them stop forever and ever.

346
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
We were on a backpacking trip

347
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:45,000
and these guys were doing the old Beavis and Butthead thing

348
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:49,000
And I had a younger assistant scoutmaster along with me

349
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:54,000
And we were sitting there kind of you know, saying

350
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:57,000
huh, they're at it again,

351
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,000
They're doing these voices and they're using all the catchphrases.

352
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
You know, I'm pretty sick of this

353
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:06,000
And, for whatever reason, we just decided to start to adopt

354
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:09,000
the Beavis and Butthead attitude and the catchphrases

355
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
and the sayings ourselves.

356
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
And it worked like a charm

357
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:17,000
Because as soon as myself and my assistant scoutmaster

358
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
started using these catchphrases

359
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:23,000
and adopting the attitude and everything like that,

360
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
the scouts dropped it

361
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,000
Like a hot potato.

362
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:29,000
Oh,

363
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:46,000
I can remember very clearly the look of utter disgust

364
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:50,000
and dismay that I received from the Beavis and Butthead adherents-

365
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:55,000
the two of them- when we started using their holy phrases

366
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:59,000
and attitude and the tone of voice.

367
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:03,000
It's a good way to stop things like that.

368
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:07,000
Just adopt it and say that it's the coolest thing you ever heard,

369
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:11,000
And the boys will drop it and never go back,

370
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
Never, ever.

371
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
And they didn't for the entire time they were in the truth.

372
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:19,000
They never went back and did the Beavis and Butthead thing.

373
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:29,000
My father was a wanderer

374
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:32,000
and he was a good man.

375
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:36,000
He was a great man as long as I could

376
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:40,000
and he gave me good things.

377
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
Never, ever.

